---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {HINDU_TEXTS} from '../../data/traditions';
const groups=[...new Set(HINDU_TEXTS.map(t=>t.family))];
---
<Base title="Vedas, Upanishads & foundational texts" description="Concise guides to the questions, essential themes, and interpretive context of Hindu philosophical texts." wing="wing-philosophy"><section class="shell page-intro"><a href="/philosophy/traditions/hindu" class="inline-link">← Hindu philosophical traditions</a><p class="eyebrow topic-kicker">THE READING ROOM / {HINDU_TEXTS.length} TEXTS & FAMILIES</p><h1>Ancient texts.<br/><em>Living questions.</em></h1><p>Essences and theories, with room for disagreement. These are starting points for reading—not complete translations or claims that every text teaches the same thing.</p><nav class="atlas-jumps" aria-label="Text families">{groups.map((g,i)=><a href={`#family-${i}`}>{g} ↓</a>)}</nav></section><section class="shell atlas-reading"><div class="atlas-question-panel"><p class="eyebrow">HOW THE MAP FITS TOGETHER</p><h2>Vedas → Upanishads → Vedānta interpretations</h2><p>The Upanishads belong to Vedic textual traditions. Vedānta is a family of philosophical interpretations, not another name for a single book. The arrows show a reading relationship, not one neat historical progression.</p><p>Śruti names revealed or “heard” authority in these traditions; smṛti names remembered tradition. Neither category makes every community’s canon or interpretation identical.</p></div></section>{groups.map((g,i)=><section class="shell section-space" id={`family-${i}`}><div class="section-heading"><h2>{g}</h2></div><div class="atlas-grid">{HINDU_TEXTS.filter(t=>t.family===g).map(t=><article id={t.id} class="atlas-card text-entry"><h3>{t.name}</h3><p>{t.essence}</p><p class="atlas-question">{t.question}</p><details><summary>Context & reading</summary><p>{t.context}</p><a href={t.source} class="inline-link">Source / text for further reading ↗</a></details></article>)}</div></section>)}</Base>
